1. Deadlock Debugging (10 Marks)
Exact Condition Causing Deadlock

The deadlock occurs because P1 and P2 acquire the resources in different orders.

P1 locks R1 first and waits for R2.
P2 locks R2 first and waits for R1.

This creates a Circular Wait condition.

Deadlock Conditions

The four Coffman conditions are satisfied:

Mutual Exclusion – Only one process can use a resource at a time.
Hold and Wait – Each process holds one resource while waiting for another.
No Preemption – Resources cannot be forcibly taken away.
Circular Wait – P1 waits for R2 while P2 waits for R1.

Hence, the system enters a deadlock.

Optimization

Use a fixed resource ordering.

Both processes should always acquire resources in the same order:

lock(R1)
lock(R2)
Critical Section
unlock(R2)
unlock(R1)

This removes the circular wait condition and eliminates deadlock.

Q2. CPU Utilization Problem (10 Marks)
Cause

The scheduler log shows:

Ready → Waiting → Ready → Waiting

This indicates that processes are frequently waiting for I/O operations.

Therefore:

CPU remains idle while processes wait for I/O.
CPU utilization drops to 30%.
Debugging

The system is I/O-bound rather than CPU-bound.

Processes spend more time waiting than executing.

Optimizations
Increase the degree of multiprogramming so another process can use the CPU while one waits.
Improve I/O performance using faster disks, SSDs, DMA, or asynchronous I/O.
Result

CPU utilization increases because the processor spends less time idle.

Q3. Memory Bottleneck Debugging (10 Marks)
Given
Code = 5 MB
Heap = 150 MB
Stack = 1 MB per thread
Threads = 25
RAM = 2 GB
Analysis

Total stack memory

= 25 × 1 MB

= 25 MB

Total memory

= Code + Heap + Stack

= 5 + 150 + 25

= 180 MB

Although total memory fits into RAM, the program experiences frequent page faults because:

Large heap causes poor memory locality.
Multiple threads increase memory accesses.
Pages are frequently swapped between RAM and memory.
Optimizations
Reduce heap usage and optimize memory allocation.
Reduce thread stack size or decrease the number of threads using a thread pool.
Result
Fewer page faults
Better memory utilization
Improved performance
Q4. Starvation Debugging (10 Marks)
Root Cause

The scheduler has three queues:

Q1 – Interactive (Highest Priority)
Q2 – System
Q3 – Batch

Process P5 is in Q3.

Whenever Q1 or Q2 receives new processes, they are executed first.

Therefore, P5 never gets CPU time.

This is called Starvation (Indefinite Blocking).

Optimization

Use Aging.

Increase the priority of P5 gradually as it waits.

Eventually P5 moves to a higher priority queue and gets CPU time.

Result
Fair scheduling
No starvation
Better response for long-waiting processes
Q5. Context-Switch Overhead Debugging (10 Marks)
Cause

Given:

Context switches = 20,000/sec
Burst time = 2 ms
Time quantum = 1 ms
High I/O frequency

Since the quantum (1 ms) is smaller than the burst time (2 ms):

Threads are preempted before completing.
Frequent I/O causes additional switches.

Hence, the CPU performs excessive context switching instead of useful work.

Optimizations

1. Increase Time Quantum

Increase quantum from 1 ms to 4–5 ms.

This reduces unnecessary preemption.

2. Reduce I/O Waiting

Use:

Asynchronous I/O
Buffering
Thread pools

to reduce waiting time and context switches.

Result
Lower context-switch overhead
Higher CPU efficiency
Improved system throughput
